eat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina remains one of the closest states on the presidential map, with a partisan lean of about one point toward Republicans. The term-limited incumbent leaves the 2028 race an open contest, removing the usual advantage a sitting president carries into a state this narrow. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, so the picture rests on state fundamentals alone.\n\nEarly coverage has focused on the primary contests rather than the fall campaign: Republicans have begun consolidating around a potential successor after a CPAC straw-poll win, while Democrats are reshaping their primary calendar. Neither development yet clarifies how a general election would play out in North Carolina. With both sides effectively starting even in a state that has decided recent presidential races by narrow margins, the race sits at the tossup line, tilting marginally toward Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}]}